exactly that!\n\nSo **I am one tile away from completing [T7,T8,T9] chow**, and I already have it in hand. Once melded, it becomes exposed if I chi, but currently I can only form it when someone discards T7/T8/T9 \u2014 but I already have them.\n\nWait \u2014 I already have T7,T8,T9 in hand. That\u2019s a complete chow! I can keep it concealed.\n\nSo my current hand contains a ready chow: [T7,T8,T9].\n\nAlso, [T1,T2,T3] is possible if I get T3.\n\n[T6,T7,T8] overlaps.\n\nStrong incentive to keep all T1,T2,T6,T7,T8,T9.\n\nThen the only discards should come from non-bamboo tiles.\n\nAvailable discards: W1, W2, W4, B3, B9, F3, J3, T1, T2, T6 \u2014 but T1/T2/T6 are valuable.\n\nSo best to discard one of the honors or dots/characters terminals.\n\nAmong W1, W2, W4: W1 and W4 are more passive. W2 is middle, but not useful.\n\nBut note: Across player has [W2,W3,W4] \u2014 they already have W2. So discarding W2 might be safe? Possibly \u2014 they may not want another W2.\n\nBut could they be making all W2? Unlikely.\n\nStill, W2 is less needed by them.\n\nBut safer to discard a tile not connected.\n\nJ3: I have one, previous player discarded F3, across discarded J3 \u2014 so J3 is out once. 3 remain. Risky? Maybe.\n\nF3: North Wind, my seat wind. But **Seat Wind** pattern gives 2 points if I have a pung of F3. But I only have one \u2014 can\u2019t make pung easily.\n\nSo negligible.\n\nBest discard: **B3** or **B9** \u2014 both dot terminals, no use, and B9 has been discarded by previous player \u2014 slightly safer.\n\nBut B3 is also safe.\n\nAlternatively, **W1** \u2014 terminal, but could be useful for Outside Hand.\n\nBut I have many options.\n\nWait \u2014 I have T2\u00d72. That can be a pair.\n\nIf I keep T2\u00d72 as pair, and build [T1,T2,T3], [T6,T7,T8], [T7,T8,T9] \u2014 conflict over T7/T8.\n\nBetter: [T1,T2,T3], [T6,T7,T8], and [T9 + ?] \u2014 no.\n\nOr: [T1,T2,T3], [T7,T8,T9], and T6 with something \u2014 T5,T6,T7?\n\nYes \u2014 [T5,T6,T7] possible.\n\nSo with draws of T3 and T5, I can form:\n- [T1,T2,T3]\n- [T5,T6,T7]\n- [T8,T9,T?] \u2014 no\n\nConflict again.\n\nBetter: [T6,T7,T8], [T7,T8,T9] \u2014 share T7,T8 \u2014 not allowed.\n\nMust choose one.\n\nSo most efficient: [T1,T2,T3] and [T7,T8,T9] \u2014 uses T1,T2,T3,T7,T8,T9 \u2014 leaves T6 as extra.\n\nThen T6 needs to be paired or used in another chow.\n\nSo T6 is somewhat loose.\n\nSimilarly, T2\u00d72 \u2014 if I use T2 in chow, I lose the pair.\n\nSo better to treat T2\u00d72 as pair, and use other tiles for chows.\n\nThen: keep T2\u00d72 as pair.\n\nForm [T6,T7,T8] and [T8,T9,X] \u2014 no.\n\nOr [T7,T8,T9] as chow, T6 as part of [T4,T5,T6]? But I don\u2019t have T4,T5.\n\nNot promising.\n\nAlternative: discard **T6** \u2014 it's the least connected.\n\nT6 doesn't help any immediate chow except [T5,T6,T7] or [T6,T7,T8], both require draws.\n\nWhereas T1,T2,T7,T8,T9 are parts of strong sequences.\n\nSo T6 is the weakest bamboo tile.\n\nSimilarly, among non-bamboo, W1,W2,W4 \u2014 W1 and W4 are terminals, W2 is simple.\n\nBut W2 is in a meld by opponent \u2014 maybe safe.\n\nHowever, safest discard is **T6** \u2014 it's a simple, not terminal, not honor, and least contributing to potential high-scoring patterns.\n\nBut let's see valid actions: I can play T6.\n\nAlternatively, **B3** \u2014 terminal, not useful.\n\nBut if I discard B3, I keep flexibility in bamboo.\n\nBut B3 is completely dead.\n\nSame with W1.\n\nBut consider: **I have two T2s** \u2014 if I discard one T2, I still have a pair? No \u2014 discard one T2 leaves one T2 \u2014 singleton.\n\nCannot do that.\n\nValid action is \"Play T2\" \u2014 meaning discard one T2? Or declare pung? No \u2014 \"Play X\" means discard tile X.\n\nSince I have two T2, playing T2 reduces it to one.\n\nBut then I lose the pair \u2014 bad.\n\nSo should not discard T2.\n\nSimilarly, T1, T7, T8, T9 \u2014 all useful for chows.\n\nT6 is the only bamboo tile not in a completed or nearly completed chow.\n\nI already have [T7,T8,T9] complete.\n\n[T1,T2,T2] \u2014 can become [T1,T2,T3] if I draw T3.\n\nT6 is isolated.\n\nSo **discard T6**.\n\nOther options:\n- **B3**: also isolated, terminal.\n- **W1**: terminal, could be useful for Outside Hand.\n\nBut Outside Hand (8 pts) requires all melds to contain terminal or honor. If I form [T7,T8,T9], that\u2019s a terminal chow \u2014 ok. [T1,T2,T3] \u2014 also terminal. Pair could be F3 or J3.\n\nSo possible.\n\nIn that case, keep W1, B3, B9, F3, J3.\n\nBut I have too many such tiles \u2014 risk getting stuck.\n\nMoreover, I\u2019d need to form pungs or chows with terminals/honors.\n\nBut I have no pungs.\n\nSo better to go for **All Simples**? Impossible \u2014 I have terminals.\n\n**Half Flush**? Only if I commit to bamboo \u2014 but I have many non-bamboo tiles.\n\nNo.\n\nBest strategy: commit to **bamboo-based chows**, minimize other suits.\n\nThus, discard non-bamboo tiles.\n\nBest candidate: **W4** \u2014 it's a simple character, not terminal, not useful (W1-W2-W4 can't form chow without W3), and W3 is taken.\n\nW4 is safer than W1 (which is terminal).\n\nSimilarly, B3 and B9 are terminals \u2014 more valuable for patterns.\n\nBut I won't pursue Outside Hand.\n\nSo discard **W4**.\n\nOr **F3** \u2014 honor, no benefit.\n\nBut F3 is my seat wind \u2014 2 points if pung. Negligible.\n\nAfter careful thought:\n\nThe tile with least future potential is **T6** \u2014 because:\n- It doesn't contribute to [T1,T2,T2] \u2192 needs T3\n- Doesn't contribute to [T7,T8,T9] (already complete)\n- To use T6, I need T4,T5 or T5,T7 or T7,T8 \u2014 all require specific draws.\n\nWhereas W1, though terminal, could be part of Outside Hand or simply discarded later.\n\nBut T6 is a simple, mid-game tile, and holding it blocks better development.\n\nHowever, reconsider: if I draw T5 and T4, I can form [T4,T5,T6], and still have [T7,T8,T9], and [T1,T2,T3] if I get T3 \u2014 but that would require three specific draws.\n\nToo optimistic.\n\nWhereas if I discard a character or dot, I simplify toward a bamboo semi-flush.\n\nBut I have only 6 bamboos \u2014 need at least 12 for full flush \u2014 impossible.\n\nSo no Full Flush.\n\nThus, best to discard a truly useless tile.\n\nFinal decision: **Discard W4**\n\nWhy?\n- It's a simple (not terminal, not honor)\n- Not part of any chow (missing W3)\n- W3 is already used in opponent's chow\n- Less valuable than W1 (which is terminal)\n- Safer than discarding a terminal that might be waited on\n\nAlternatively, **B3** \u2014 similar.\n\nBut B3 is terminal \u2014 could be useful.\n\nW4 is purely dead.\n\nAlso, across player has W2,W3,W4 \u2014 they already have W4, so unlikely to need another \u2014 safe discard.\n\nThus, **Play W4**.\n\nAnswer is in valid actions: \"Play W4\" is listed.\n\nReason: W4 is a stranded simple tile in Characters, cannot form a chow without W3 (which is likely scarce and already used), and keeping it hinders focus on developing stronger Bamboo sequences like [T1,T2,T3] and [T7,T8,T9]. Discarding it streamlines the hand toward potential Mixed or Pure patterns involving terminals.\n\nSituation: My hand is fragmented, but I hold a complete chow [T7,T8,T9] and a near-chow [T1,T2,T2] needing T3. I also have several singletons in other suits and honors. Opponent has exposed [W2,W3,W4], consuming W3. T9 has been discarded twice, signaling danger.\n\nEvaluation: Key opportunities include pursuing Mixed Straight (using T7-T8-T9, and forming 123 and 456 in other suits) or focusing on Outside Hand. However, the strongest immediate path is to prioritize viable chows in Bamboo and shed unrelated tiles. W4 is unconnected, not a terminal or honor, and its suit is partially blocked by opponents\u2019 melds.\n\nReason: W4 is the least useful tile in my hand\u2014neither a terminal nor an honor, and unable to form a chow without a scarce W3. Discarding it improves hand efficiency and avoids cluttering toward higher-value patterns.\n\nAnswer: Play W4"}
