d contribute to Outside Hand pattern if kept. Discarding one breaks incomplete structure.\n- **Play F2**: This is South Wind (prevalent wind). Pung of F2 would score \"Prevalent Wind\" (2 points), but I only have one F2. Discarding it loses potential for that pattern. However, holding it risks giving others points. Since F2 appears four times unshown, likely none are out yet\u2014so danger is high.\n- **Play W2**: I have two W2s. The across player already has a chow [W2,W3,W4] and could be waiting on W2 for a pair or even a second pung. Only one W2 remains unshown\u2014others are accounted for (one in their chow, two in my hand, one possibly discarded earlier). So only one W2 left unseen\u2014likely already gone. Thus, playing W2 is relatively safe.\n- **Play J3**: I have two J3s (White Dragon), and two remain unshown. Others have discarded J3s (e.g., across player played J3 early), so likely not building White Dragon pung. No one has shown dragon melds. Playing J3 might be safe. However, keeping both J3s gives me a pair, which could serve as an eye for winning.\n\nBest possible patterns:\n- If I can form a pair eye (e.g., W2, J3, or F2), and complete other chows, I might aim for **Half Flush** (honors + one suit) or **All Chows** (if all melds are chows). Currently, I have two chows and one pung ([T2,T2,T2])\u2014so **All Chows** is impossible unless the pung is reinterpreted, but it's already melded via Peng\u2014so fixed.\n- Alternatively, **Mixed Triple Chow** or **Pure Shifted Chows** not feasible due to mixed suits.\n- More realistically, **Melded Hand** (6 points) is possible since all my melds are exposed (Peng T2, Chi T6, Chi T5), and if I win by discard, this scores. Also, **Dragon Pung** (J3 pair or pung?) \u2014 but I don\u2019t have a pung of dragons, only a pair potential.\n- Holding J3 pair and drawing another J3 would give pung \u2192 2 points (Dragon Pung). But only two J3s left unshown\u2014low chance.\n- Better: Win quickly with existing pair. Best candidate eyes: J3 (safe), W2 (possible), F2 (risky due to prevalent wind).\n\nReason: I should discard a tile that is least useful to opponents and maintains my chance to win. \n- T5 is a simple tile, not terminal/honor, but I have only one\u2014no pair. It doesn't help any potential eye. However, discarding it keeps W2x2, B1, B2, F2, J3x2\u2014which gives multiple pair options.\n- But B1 and B2 are both terminals, and together form a partial chow (B1-B2-B3), but B3 is endangered (used in across\u2019s meld). Moreover, B1 and B2 are vulnerable to \"Outside Hand\" or \"Lower Tiles\" for others.\n- F2 is dangerous\u2014prevalent wind, and someone may be waiting on it.\n- W2: Two in hand, one unshown. Across player used W2 in chow, but no sign they are collecting it. Likely safe.\n- J3: White Dragon, no one has shown interest. Discarded by multiple players. Safest honor to keep or discard.\n\nHowever, **I must choose a discard from valid actions**. To maximize safety and maintain flexibility, **discarding T5** removes a non-pair, non-terminal, non-honor tile, keeping all potential pair candidates (W2, J3, F2) and preserving chances to win with a better eye.\n\nAlternatively, **discarding B1 or B2** removes a terminal, reducing chance for \u201cOutside Hand\u201d but also removing weak tile. But if I keep both, I could form B1-B2-B3 chow\u2014but B3 is scarce.\n\nGiven that **Chi T6** was in my history, meaning I formed [T5,T6,T7] from a discard, and now I have T5 in hand, it's redundant.\n\nBut wait: my meld is [T5,T6,T7], so the T5 there is fixed. The T5 in hand is separate? Wait\u2014hand tiles list says T5:1. Melds: [T5,T6,T7] \u2014 so total T5 count: at least one in meld, one in hand \u2192 two T5s. But unshown T5:2 \u2014 total four copies. Previous player has [T4,T5,T6] and [T5,T5,T5] \u2014 that\u2019s three in their pung, one in chow \u2192 four T5s already used! So **no T5 remains unshown** \u2192 the T5 in my hand must be incorrect?\n\nWait: unshown T5:2 \u2014 contradiction.\n\nLet\u2019s reconcile:\n- Total T5: 4 copies.\n- Previous player has: [T4,T5,T6] (1 T5) and [T5,T5,T5] (3 T5s) \u2192 total 4 T5s.\n\u2192 So **all T5s are accounted for**.\n\u2192 Therefore, I cannot have a T5 in hand.\n\nThis suggests a data inconsistency. But assuming game state is correct, perhaps my \u201cT5\u201d in hand is a mistake? Or the meld [T5,T6,T7] uses one T5, and I have another? But previous player has four T5s \u2192 impossible.\n\nTherefore, **the T5 in my hand must be invalid** \u2014 likely a display error. But since the system lists it as a valid action (\"Play T5\"), we must assume it's present.\n\nPossibility: Kong promotion? No record.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the previous player\u2019s [T5,T5,T5] is wrong? But their history shows: Play T5, then later Peng W5, then Chi T5 \u2014 so they claimed T5 to form a chow [T4,T5,T6], and earlier had a pung [T5,T5,T5]? That would require 6 T5s \u2014 impossible.\n\nAh! Error in interpretation: melds of previous player: [[T4,T5,T6],[W5,W5,W5],[B5,B5,B5]] \u2014 so [T4,T5,T6] is one chow \u2192 uses one T5. They do **not** have a pung of T5. Earlier, they did **Chi T5**, meaning they used a discarded T5 to complete [T4,T5,T6]. And they never showed a T5 pung.\n\nBut in their meld list, only [T4,T5,T6] \u2014 one T5.\n\nAnd my meld: [T5,T6,T7] \u2014 one T5.\n\nSo total T5 used: 2.\n\nUnshown: 2 \u2192 so two T5s still available.\n\nMy hand has one T5 \u2192 possible.\n\nNo conflict.\n\nBack to decision.\n\nBest move: **Discard T5**.\n\nWhy?\n- T5 is a simple (non-terminal, non-honor) tile, not contributing to any pair.\n- Keeping W2x2 gives a potential pair; F2 is prevalent wind \u2014 risky to discard; J3x2 is a good pair candidate; B1/B2 are terminals but could form part of a chow.\n- T5 is least valuable for pairing and not needed elsewhere.\n- Also, **All Simples** (2 points) is possible if I avoid terminals/honors. But I have F2 and J3 \u2014 honors \u2014 so **All Simples** is broken anyway.\n- **Half Flush** (W+H) possible if I go for character suit, but I only have scattered W tiles.\n- My strongest potential is **Melded Hand** (6 points) + possibly **Dragon Pung** (2) if I get third J3, or **Prevalent Wind** if I get more F2s. But I only have one F2.\n\nBetter to keep pair candidates.\n\nThus, **discard T5** to reduce dead weight.\n\nAnswer: Play T5"}
